    27	## The nine steps
    28	
    29	### 1. Reconcile the brief and define design success
    30	
    31	Read the current step from the brief. Freeze a source snapshot with stable brief line IDs, page/template inventory, audience, principal job, conversion or task goal, known constraints and decision status. Resolve repository, stack, design tool, roles and languages from settings. Write observable success criteria for each visitor task. If a brief, rules file, authoritative role or essential fact is missing, record the gap and its owner; do not substitute a guessed fact.
    32	
    33	Maintain a discovery ledger for audience needs, current workflows, pain points and constraints. Record evidence source/date, assumptions, confidence, design consequence and risk. Use supplied or already-authorized research, support and analytics evidence. Assign material assumptions an owner and validation method in Step 5. Never present brief assertions or visual references as user research; preserve existing contact and payment approvals. The `design-research` skill keeps this ledger next to its reference findings, and Step 5 checks it (`discovery-evidence`).
    34	
    35	Write one proposed visual thesis: “For [audience] doing [job], the design makes [brief-backed distinction] visible through [hierarchy/content behavior].” This is internal rationale, not website copy. Cite the exact brief lines for each clause. Specify two or three operational consequences, such as making the next action prominent or putting evidence beside a decision. A decorative adjective alone is not a point of view. Exit when the source hierarchy, scope and decision map are unambiguous enough for content work.
    36	
    37	### 2. Visitor needs, content and evidence
    38	
    39	Copy comes before layout. Take approved copy from the brief or the project's writer; preserve exact approved wording. Build the copy deck with a stable slot ID, screen/state, purpose, text, language/direction, character range, tone note, source lines and all fact references. Every claim, statistic, named customer, logo and quotation needs a confirmed project fact and any required consent. Source presence alone is not confirmation. Record who checked it, when and against which source revision. A testimonial also needs its exact wording, speaker and permission.
    40	
    41	Use visible `[placeholder]` for a missing line, listing its slot, owner and required source. Never create a plausible headline, number or customer as temporary content. Placeholders may support an explicitly incomplete wireframe; they fail Step 9. Give the writer a text-fit request when a slot is too long; widen or reflow the layout first. Do not cut copy, ellipsize content or invent a shorter claim to fit a box.
    42	
    43	Collect image briefs describing the communication job, required content, rights and alt-text intent. Identify what not to show. Exit with an inventory that accounts for every slot, source, missing item and consent requirement. Known blocking content gaps remain visible.
    44	
    45	### 3. Information architecture
    46	
    47	Map each page and section to a visitor task and an exact brief line. Each section brief names its purpose, content slots, evidence, CTA and destination, expected state changes and success check. Remove sections with no job. A structural decision, grid break, illustration or motion proposal gets the same trace, so the critic can reject anything without one.
    48	
    49	Build a focused reference board of three real, inspected references: one useful for hierarchy, one for content density/type and one for the relevant interaction. These are comparison roles, not required vendors or aesthetic templates. Record URL, inspection date, local evidence where rights permit, what works, what would be wrong for this project and the relevant brief lines. Do not copy a reference's text, assets or trademark presentation. If a reference was inaccessible, mark it untested and replace it before the critic relies on it.
    50	
    51	Navigation model and findability: Specify navigation labels, grouping/order, destinations, current-location cues and back/escape behavior. Document discovery and movement between navigation levels. Specify search, filter and sort behavior when tasks require them; otherwise justify N/A. In Step 5, test finding priority destinations from realistic entry points using the proposed labels; record wrong turns and revisions. The Step 5 result is recorded as `navigation-findability`.
    52	
    53	Exit with the complete sitemap, navigation model, task flows, section briefs and a single recommended direction. Reference selection must support the project's point of view, not impose a fashionable palette.
    54	
    55	### 4. Grayscale desktop wireframes
    56	
    57	Lay out the full desktop page/template set at 1440 CSS px using the real copy deck, clear reading order and numbered states. Explore two structural options for genuinely open sections unless the brief specifies otherwise; the separate three-to-five variant loop does not require duplicating every page. Keep all options grayscale and type-neutral at this phase.
    58	
    59	Annotate column spans, reading order, grouping, content-driven height, intended media job, interaction/state behavior and brief references. Check the project's other permitted desktop widths. A centered section means its principal heading, copy and CTA are compositionally centered as a unit; centered images or cells alone do not count. Permit at most one such section per page. Do not evade the count by replacing semantic sections with anonymous containers.
    60	
    61	At Step 4, check grayscale structure, copy and provenance and record issues for testing. The complete wireframe quality gate runs within the existing Step 5 gate (below), not here. No centered three-icon row, vague hero over three cards, arbitrary KPI row, icon soup or unsourced data survives structural review. A placeholder or missing decision is listed explicitly. Exit with the complete grayscale set and testable issues, not a polished home page alone.
    62	
    63	### 5. Test and approve the desktop wireframes
    64	
    65	Use the existing Step 5 gate as the explicit boundary before color and type work. No new approval gate is added. Test whether each audience can identify the purpose, find the relevant evidence and complete the primary task. Record tasks, actual participants or review method, observed failures and limitations; do not invent user research. The QA gate checks structure and accessibility intent. The writer checks copy and claim sources. The critic reviews structure and copy together against the brief and real reference work.
    66	
    67	Before testing, define each priority task's neutral scenario, realistic starting point and observable completion criterion. Record participant/reviewer context, unaided completion, wrong turns, assistance and severity. Do not demonstrate the intended path first. Distinguish representative-user testing from expert walkthroughs and record remaining assumptions. Retest failed critical tasks after revisions against the new version. Resolve material uncertainty within the existing Step 5 approval gate. The record is `task-test`.
    68	
    69	Within the existing Step 5 gate, after the task and navigation-findability tests and necessary retests, run the complete wireframe quality gate; require PASS before structure approval and Step 6. It checks structure, copy, provenance and placeholders, and its manual records carry the real Step 5 results (`task-test`, `navigation-findability`); it records unresolved visual checks (`accent-area` and `palette` always; `emphasis-order`, `type-measure`, `colour-roles` and `grid-per-width` when not yet judged in grayscale) as deferred to Step 6, never as passed. It is a run inside the existing gate, not a new approval gate. A wireframe PASS covers every declared desktop screen and locale. Each rendering must declare an existing page and receive the applicable structural/copy, accessibility, integrity and manual checks; missing coverage blocks structure approval. Each screen declares a default-state rendering per declared language (`screens[].states`), and the manual records cover every screen in every declared language.
    70	
    71	Fix critical task failures and all structural/copy BLOCK findings, then obtain explicit approval for the complete scoped desktop wireframe set with version IDs. Approval of one page does not approve its siblings or templates. If unresolved copy or a consequential question prevents a meaningful structure decision, the gate stays On hold. This is where the no-AI-look checks run before styling; they run again on the final visual version.
    72	
    73	### 6. Desktop visual design
    74	
    75	Only after Step 5 approval, develop the chosen point of view with type, spacing, color, assets and motion. Start with the project's approved tokens and components. A new brand token still requires the existing approval. Pair display and reading roles deliberately; a single family with a justified role contrast is valid. Select fonts only after checking the project's language coverage, weight availability, licence and real text rendering.
    76	
    77	Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail. The shared inventory is the handoff's `components` list (one definition per component: variants, states, token bindings and behaviour); every `screens[].components` entry references it by id. It is judged structurally in wireframes and fully at handoff, and recorded as `component-consistency`; the critic compares the instances across screens.
    78	
    79	Use at most **six distinct rendered type sizes per page**: display, title, section heading, body, secondary text and label. Six supplies a useful hierarchy without arbitrary intermediate steps; fewer is preferable when sufficient. Document font family, fallback, size, line height, weight and tracking for each role. Weight and spacing carry distinctions before another size is added. Derive the sizes from a documented scale (a ratio or an explicit step rationale) and record the measure: body text runs about 45 to 75 characters per line at the reference width, body line height is at least 1.5 (a project rule), the WCAG 2.2 text-spacing overrides (line height 1.5, paragraph spacing 2, letter spacing 0.12 and word spacing 0.16 times the font size; SC 1.4.12) cause no loss of content or function, and long-form text never spans the full 1440 px. Check the scale in every required script, including numerals and mixed-direction strings; a script that needs a larger optical size gets it within the same ceiling. The six-size ceiling applies within each locale; translations may use a separately approved metric-equivalent scale with the same ceiling. Text must remain readable at user zoom. The scale, measure and line-height result is recorded as `type-measure`.
    80	
    81	Use a named spacing scale and specify the grid, margins, gutters, alignment lines and section rhythm in tokens. For each width in `settings.desktopWidths`, specify the column count, gutter, outer margin, maximum content width and how sections recompose between widths (what stays aligned, what grows, where the measure caps). Column counts are positive unitless integers recorded beside the grid spec, not spacing tokens. Gutters, outer margins and maximum content widths reference approved dimension tokens with units (bare 0 allowed, no negative spacing tokens). Document recomposition between widths as behaviour. The result is recorded as `grid-per-width`. A layout that is only correct at 1440 px is incomplete. Every intentional grid break identifies the brief-backed reason and the reading-order consequence. Do not fabricate irregularity to appear human. Use neutrals and one strong accent. Define colour as named roles in the token JSON before use: page and raised surfaces, primary and secondary text, borders and dividers, the one accent, focus indicator and each semantic state (success, warning, error, information), each with its value and the job it serves. Build the contrast matrix at token level for every text-on-surface and non-text pair the screens use, including hover, focus, selected, error and disabled appearances (disabled text is exempt from the contrast minimum but must still be recognisable), and use only pairs that pass the WCAG 2.2 AA floor. A colour not in the role list does not appear on a screen. The role list and contrast matrix are recorded as `colour-roles`. The accent rule: accent-bearing area must be at most ten percent in both the full-page composition and the reviewed desktop viewport. Semantic colors may communicate states but require text or shape as well; they are not extra decorative accents. The screenshot review catches color inside media and effects that DOM measurements miss.
    82	
    83	Before styling and again on the visual version, record each screen's intended emphasis order (the first, second and third thing a visitor should read) and its single primary action per viewport; at most one element per viewport carries the strongest emphasis. Verify the order on a 1440 px screenshot viewed small and blurred (and in grayscale at Step 4/5); when the visible order differs from the intended one, change size, weight, position, contrast or space, not the copy. Record the result in the `craft` artifact and as `emphasis-order`.
    84	
    85	Avoid a default purple-blue combination, generic gradient hero, stock glass effects or repeated rounded-card grid. An existing brand conflict is reported for a decision, not silently rebranded. Each visual and icon must help someone understand, navigate, decide or recognize something. Remove it when that job cannot be named.
    86	
    87	For each motion, record the job, trigger, duration in milliseconds, easing, affected elements, interruption/cleanup behavior and static fallback. Under reduced motion there is no movement. Essential content remains visible with JavaScript disabled; the project's specified script-failure deadline is an acceptance check. Motion has no value merely because a page feels quiet without it.
    88	
    89	Render every required language and direction with actual writer-approved copy, then perform a clearly labelled synthetic long-text stress test at 150 percent of the slot's typical length. This synthetic text is test input, never publishable copy. Character ranges are planning guidance: if an approved translation exceeds one, revise the range and layout rather than truncate. Use logical start/end layout rules; check mixed numbers, punctuation, links and directional icons in RTL. Never mirror logos or text blindly. Exit with approved desktop visuals, complete state behavior and no blocking craft, copy or accessibility defects.
    90	
    91	### 7. Mobile and tablet
    92	
    93	This step is retained unchanged in the sequence. It may begin only after the approver approves desktop and the brief explicitly allows it. Until then its status is deferred, not passed, and no mobile/tablet layouts are made. For an approved desktop-only delivery, the brief must record that this step is outside the current scope; that record is not a mobile approval.
    94	
    95	When authorized, derive smaller layouts and navigation from the same copy, tasks and tokens. Document reflow and touch behavior without relying on hover. Test all key journeys and relevant states. The current rewrite does not authorize that phase.
    96	
    97	### 8. Validate, document and final design approval
    98	
    99	Freeze the complete scoped design: screens and applicable states, copy, tokens, assets, motion, semantic outline, SEO/schema intent, accessibility evidence and issue list. Run the integrated pre-handoff checker and the independent critic against this exact revision. Each result is PASS or FAIL with evidence; untested means FAIL for a required BLOCK check. A WARN includes its impact, owner and disposition and cannot hide an open implementation question.
   100	
   101	Validate WCAG 2.2 AA with automated and manual evidence. Include contrast pairs, keyboard and focus sequence, target sizes/exceptions, alt text, heading order, standalone action labels, dragging alternatives, authentication behavior where applicable, text resizing, reflow, RTL and screen-reader behavior. Automated axe and focus heuristics do not prove conformance. Resolve incomplete automated results through a documented manual check. One H1 per page is this package's audit contract, not a claim that WCAG universally mandates that count.
   102	
   103	The approver signs the final screen/state versions and hashes. A conditional design review may be recorded for history, but a pending claim, consent, asset, question or blocking result prevents Step 9. No AI reviewer supplies human approval.
   104	
   105	### 9. Handoff to the project's engineer
   106	
   107	Create the fixed package described in `references/handoff-contract.md`. Its canonical record is `handoff.json`; `HANDOFF.md` is the generated readable checklist. The designer, writer, engineer and QA gate verify the same criterion IDs and artifact hashes. Do not maintain competing lists with different acceptance rules.
   108	
   109	The package contains the private design link and exact frame names; DESIGN.md; project settings; DTCG-style token JSON and generated CSS with the actual build script/config and dependency lock; every screen's component/state inventory; versioned 1440-pixel PNG frames; a complete copy deck with slot placement screenshots; sourced/licensed assets; motion behavior; BLOCK/WARN checks; WCAG evidence; heading outline; image alt intent; standalone action labels; and page-specific SEO/schema intent. It also includes the critic record, tool evidence and the approver's signed record. Unknown schema eligibility is an open question, not invented structured data.
   110	
   111	Zero unresolved questions and zero placeholders are required. Each non-applicable state or audit criterion needs a reason and evidence, not an empty cell. The engineer acknowledges package completeness or identifies the exact missing criterion; Fannan fixes it before resubmission. Prototypes stay reference only. The engineer builds in the repository and stack in settings, the QA gate reports on the specific preview and commit SHA, and the approver follows the existing merge rule. A green PR or chat assertion is not a QA pass; missing proof fails, and a report on another SHA does not transfer.
   112	
   113	If any input fails, do not send a partial handoff. Record its owner and next action, set the task to Stuck or On hold and notify the approver through the already-authorized channel. Sending still requires the existing approval; this document does not send anything.
   114	
   115	## The screen-production loop inside the permitted step
   116	
   117	Keep the existing loop: **precheck → short plan and approval → Shape → Explore → Implement reference design → See → independent Critic → Harden → Review → design-share**. The plan states goal, permitted step, screens, checks and cost. Explore three to five private variants on one genuinely open dimension and have the approver choose; during wireframing the variants stay grayscale. Only the chosen direction advances. Do not generate options after a documented direction is already approved without an issue that reopens it.
   118	
   119	The critic is separate and read-only. It scores copy and design together using `references/critic-rubric.md` and records PASS/FAIL per check with a source or annotated frame. Internal zero-to-four scores track improvement; they cannot average away a failed hard rule. Stop after at most three rounds, or earlier when the total gains less than one point between rounds. On either stop, send the approver the scores, fixed defects, remaining severities and recommended next action. The cap never turns a failure into a pass and never permits a fourth round without a new authorized decision.
   120	
   121	## Folding in the approver's feedback
   122	
   123	Keep one feedback log per project, beside the issue list (`feedback-log`). Each item records an ID, the approver's exact words, date and channel, the screen/state/version it concerns, Fannan's interpretation, the decision (apply, clarify or conflict), the change made, the new version that carries it and the approver's confirmation. Ask a clarifying question when an item is ambiguous instead of guessing; an item that would break the WCAG 2.2 AA floor, a project rule, a confirmed fact or an approved earlier decision is reported as a conflict with the options, never applied silently. Re-present only after every item is resolved or explicitly deferred by the approver, and show what changed against the previous version. Feedback is scoped to the project and step where it was given. A preference that looks reusable is proposed to Fadel as a memory save; it is applied to other projects only after his written yes, and it never overrides a project's own rules.
   124	
   125	## Runtime, integrity and activation
   126	
   127	Keep the existing tool set and exact dependency versions. Choose the assignment's explicit **reviewed Node 22 upgrade** route for the original Node 20 host; the scripts reject unsupported runtimes rather than pretending compatibility. The proposed target is Node **22.23.3**, Linux x64, `node-v22.23.3-linux-x64.tar.xz`, SHA256 `df450af89261115ef9f9e3830c3eeb2cc9213b63c720b1af623cb5dcbe2e02de`; the versioned official checksum record is captured in `runtime/node-runtime.json`. Confirm target architecture and runtime compatibility, then obtain the existing Amin review and Fadel install approval before installing it alongside the old runtime. This Mac's Node 22.22.3 supports local tests, not proof of that upgrade. Install npm dependencies outside the manifest-covered release tree with the lockfile and `--ignore-scripts`. Every dependency consumer verifies the candidate's reviewed lock and installed-tree byte inventory before loading dependency code. An environment variable chooses the location, never the trusted bytes; changed target-platform bytes need a fresh inventory and Amin review before use. The complete skill/tool candidate, caller updates and rename map travel in one release with a new external `MANIFEST.sha256` and pin.
   128	
   129	Follow `references/security-and-install.md` for review, staging, backup, verification, atomic activation and rollback. All nine workflow skills and seven vendor skills are included. Historical old names remain only in the immutable source pack and the explicit migration map. The helper's existing filename and data path are not silently renamed. Unsupported media discovery/resume commands are removed from design-media rather than added to an unavailable helper.
   130	
   131	The four project roles are generic throughout the candidate. Proposed memory saves are supplied as a file; no live memory is updated. Save 8 contains the exact paid-action sentence above. The two deleted routines stay deleted; their promised verbatim archive is not in this pack, so its contents and preservation are untested.
   132	
   133	## Answers to the three latest questions
   134	
   135	**Matching avatars:** the rule is enforceable as an acceptance contract, not a guarantee from a generative model. Read the project-owned avatar registry first: each bot has one reserved animal identity and **no two bots share an animal**. Check the entire registered family for duplicates before generation and again before acceptance; a missing registry or duplicate assignment blocks the batch. Inspect the existing reference images, freeze their file hashes and define crop, camera angle, head scale, background, lighting, material/line treatment, palette and expression range. Use the same approved references and settings through the selected provider's available reference/style controls, and one approved anchor avatar for the batch. Record seed/model/version where the provider exposes them; a repeated seed is not a promise of identical style. Generate only after Fadel approves the specific paid job and any upload. Compare a contact sheet with references at equal size; reject inconsistent members rather than changing the style halfway through. No references were supplied here, so no avatar generation or matching test is claimed.
   136	
   137	**Gate before styling:** yes, make the existing Step 5 exit explicit. Run structural/copy checks on Step 4 grayscale output, fix failures, and obtain complete-set approval at Step 5 before Step 6. This strengthens the evidence at the existing boundary without changing the nine steps or adding an approval gate.
   138	
   139	**One Step 9 checklist:** yes. Maintain one canonical `handoff.json`, render its `HANDOFF.md`, and attach designer and QA results to the same versioned criteria. QA adds implementation evidence against the build SHA; it does not rewrite design acceptance. Any change to the package invalidates the affected evidence and approval until reviewed.
   140	
   141	## Threat model for the pre-handoff gate
   142	
   143	Pages are authored by Fannan or a human designer: honest but possibly sloppy, not an attacker. The gate must catch honest mistakes and ordinary authoring patterns completely (body or html clipping, zero-size or `display: contents` wrappers, open shadow roots, frames, objects and embeds, stylesheets loaded from `data:` or `blob:` URLs, state pages), and must fail closed on anything it cannot measure (closed shadow roots, unreadable or unserved stylesheets, frames, a vector field or native-function check that could not run, JavaScript-off content that is not equivalent).
   144	
   145	Deliberate obfuscation is outside the main threat model: for example JavaScript string escapes, comment tricks inside strings, patching `Function.prototype`, or base64-encoded stylesheets. Cheap defences are applied where they are simple (escapes are decoded and raw text is matched with and without comment stripping; the reduced-motion counter binds the native `requestAnimationFrame` at install and `call`/`apply`/`bind`/`Reflect.apply` are in the native-function check; `data:`/`blob:` stylesheets are refused). Anything beyond that is listed in `UNTESTED.md` as a known limit and is raised as ENH, not P0-P2, in future rounds. Defence in depth stays: computed-style checks, the JavaScript-off pass and the source scan.
   146	
   147	## Review convergence rule
   148	
   149	A review round passes when there are zero P0, P1 and P2 findings; P3 and ENH items are batched and don't block.
   150	
   151	## Assumptions and evidence boundaries
   152	
   153	Six type sizes, a strict ten-percent accent threshold and a 150-percent long-text stress case are design-policy choices made for this assignment, not scientific detectors of AI authorship. The Impeccable detector's findings are advisory; successful execution is not a design-quality PASS. Card-grid, generic-hero, dashboard-trope and decoration judgments require the scoped independent critic evidence in the same gate. English/Arabic filler and common three-part headline scans provide additional screening; semantic review in every project language remains mandatory. Passing these checks removes known bad patterns; it cannot establish that a human would endorse the result. The independent critic and approver remain essential.
   154	
   155	A **labelled example project** (the example fixture in the supplied review pack, `input/fannan-review-pack/example-project-*`) is used only as a dry-run of the workflow, in `references/example-project-check.md`. Its draft rules, absent authoritative brief/fact register and missing role/stack mappings are detected as blockers. Its project claims are not transplanted into this plan or promoted to confirmed facts.
   156	
   157	All local command results and negative-path checks are in the separate verification record. Anything not executed is in the untested list. Historical test claims stay historical. This candidate does not create Amin's review, live credentials, branch enforcement, provider access, a real approved design or a successful live handoff.
     1	# Critic rubric — copy and design together
     2	
     3	The critic is independent and read-only. Load the frozen brief, rules, settings, copy/fact ledgers, reference board, scoped screen/state renders and integrated quality evidence. Do not start from the designer's self-assessment. Evaluate each page and all scoped states/locales against their real reference work; check a composition both at full-page scale and at readable 1440-pixel size. Missing evidence is FAIL, not benefit of the doubt.
     4	
     5	## Score anchors and pass rule
     6	
     7	Use zero-to-four scores only to make iteration visible: **0** absent or contradicted; **1** recognizable intent with major defects; **2** functional but generic or materially incomplete; **3** clear, specific and supported with all required checks passing; **4** unusually resolved craft, supported by concrete comparisons and detail. A score of four needs an explanation, not enthusiasm.
     8	
     9	Every row and every page receives a public **PASS or FAIL**, a score, an artifact reference and a reason. PASS requires at least 3 in every applicable row, all hard checks passing and no open P0/P1. Scores are never averaged to compensate for a failed dimension. Each row has an ID (in brackets in the table). In the handoff record every critic copy or design check names its row in `row`, and the final round carries at least one passing, evidenced check for each of the eight rows; a missing row fails the contract. A WARN can remain only with a named owner and explicit disposition and no unanswered build question.
    10	
    11	| Dimension | PASS at 3 requires | Evidence / automatic failure |
    12	|---|---|---|
    13	| Brief and point of view (`brief-point-of-view`) | One legible idea serving this audience/job, expressed consistently across the page | Brief lines for every section and deliberate decision; any orphan section fails |
    14	| Copy specificity and truth (`copy-truth`) | Accurate approved wording, identifiable offering/task and confirmed proof | Slot/source/claim ledger, project-wording checks; transferable headline, invented proof or filler fails |
    15	| Content hierarchy and task flow (`hierarchy-task-flow`) | Reading order, emphasis and CTA help the named task; first impression matches actual content; the recorded emphasis order holds on the blurred screenshot | Task walkthrough, neutral task-test record and annotated frame; vague hero over icon cards or misleading primary action fails |
    16	| Typography and language (`typography-language`) | Deliberate role pairing, at most six sizes from a documented scale, a body measure of about 45 to 75 characters, body line height of at least 1.5, no loss under the WCAG text-spacing overrides, correct scripts/directions and uncut translations | Token-to-DOM mapping, language renders, long-text/zoom checks; clipping or missing script support fails |
    17	| Spacing, rhythm and grid (`spacing-rhythm-grid`) | Repeatable spacing roles with purposeful contrast in density; columns, gutters, margins and maximum width specified for every declared desktop width; any grid break aids the brief | Alignment/grid overlay and section-to-section comparison; random spacing/novelty fails |
    18	| Color, imagery and restraint (`colour-imagery-restraint`) | Named colour roles with a token-level contrast matrix, legible neutral base, one accent within area budget, useful licensed imagery, no default effects | Measured color roles, contrast, screenshot, asset jobs; palette/card/gradient/glass rule violation fails |
    19	| Interaction, states and motion (`interaction-states-motion`) | Every applicable state and recovery path clear, including reachable states beyond the seven and form behavior; motion explains change and has static fallback | State matrix, keyboard story, reduced-motion/no-JS proof; an unspecified reachable state, transition or applicable form behavior fails, as do fake live behavior and color-only status |
    20	| Accessibility and handoff precision (`accessibility-handoff`) | Applicable WCAG 2.2 AA criteria supported and each implementation choice specified | Automated plus manual evidence and canonical checklist; missing evidence, open question or unmet AA fails |
    21	
    22	For a wireframe review, typography means hierarchy/readability, color means grayscale restraint, and interaction means state intent. Record color/font/motion polish as **deferred to Step 6**, outside that phase's score. Do not reward visual polish before structure is approved. The handoff rubric covers all rows without those deferrals.
    23	
    24	## Cross-screen component comparison
    25	
    26	In every round, compare the equivalent instances of each reused component across all screens in scope against its shared definition: anatomy, variant, size, token bindings, content constraints, states and usage. Record the screens compared, each difference with its reason or defect, and a PASS or FAIL in the final round's `componentComparison`. An unexplained difference fails `component-consistency` and the relevant rubric row.
    27	
    28	## Specificity test and reference comparison
    29	
    30	For each page, quote its actual hero line from the approved copy deck, mask the brand name and compare its meaning with two unrelated business categories. If both could use it unchanged, mark `generic-hero` FAIL. A factual descriptor, named audience job or specific proposition must make the line belong to this brief. Do not invent competitor quotations. The writer owns revisions and existing approval still applies.
    31	
    32	Compare each page with the three inspected reference works selected in Step 3. State the exact aspect being compared, the reference evidence, where this design falls short and how the brief justifies a deliberate difference. “Looks premium” or “feels human” is not a criterion. No reference URL may be presented as inspected unless it was opened and reviewed. The current candidate contains a rubric, not a fabricated comparison against an unbuilt project.
    33	
    34	## Defects and the bounded loop
    35	
    36	P0 means a serious truth, safety, access or essential task failure; P1 means a broken required rule, missing critical evidence or a build-blocking omission. P2/P3 are lesser issues only when they do not violate a BLOCK check. A required check cannot be downgraded to make a page pass.
    37	
    38	At each round record screen/state hashes, every score and PASS/FAIL, defects with severity, evidence, owner and next correction. The designer revises; the critic evaluates the new artifact independently. Stop at three rounds maximum, or earlier when total improvement is less than one point between rounds. The stop packet lists all round scores, what changed, open defects and the decision needed from the approver. It does not authorize handoff, a fourth round or relaxed thresholds. An approval cannot waive the WCAG floor, factual truth or security rules.
    39	
    40	The final verdict is either **PASS for the explicitly listed scope/version** or **FAIL — return to the relevant step**. A draft, an unavailable reference or a capped loop is never “pass with conditions” for Step 9.
     1	# Step 9 package contract
     2	
     3	`handoff.json` is the authored source of truth. `HANDOFF.md` is its readable checklist, generated by the checker. Keep one set of criterion IDs; do not maintain a second design/QA acceptance list. Freeze the source JSON, reference frames and all linked evidence by version/hash. Any affected change requires fresh checks and approval. Paths in the JSON are resolved from the command's working directory; use absolute paths for release evidence to avoid ambiguity.
     4	
     5	This contract specifies what each real project must supply. The included synthetic fixture is only an executable checker example. It is not a real project's copy, factual proof, approval, licensed asset set or completed handoff.
     6	
     7	## Fixed package contents
     8	
     9	| Artifact | Required contents and exact acceptance |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| `handoff.json` and generated `HANDOFF.md` | Version 1 record; project/phase; no open questions; no placeholders; all screens, locales and states; one criterion ID per check; actual evidence and owner/reviewer |
