    30	
    31	`tools/handoff-contract.mjs` is the executable validator. Use its field names exactly; do not infer an unvalidated schema from prose. The following is its authoring guide:
    32	
    33	- `version`, `phase`, `brief.path`, `rules.path`, and `settings` identify the contract and inputs. Settings provides `path`, `repository`, `stack` and `roles.engineer`, `roles.qaGate`, `roles.writer`, `roles.approver`, matching the actual project settings. Fannan copies those values; it never selects a stack. `settings.desktopWidths` lists the project's declared desktop CSS widths: unique positive integers that always include the canonical 1440 (for example `[1440]` or `[1440, 1280]`); a missing, duplicate or 1440-less list fails the contract in both phases (the wireframe is a desktop handoff too; there is no default list). It declares widths to check; it does not authorize a mobile phase.
    34	- `pointOfView.idea` and `pointOfView.briefRefs` establish the visual thesis. A separate decision/section ledger links every section, grid break, token and expressive element to source lines and the relevant audience task.
    35	- `languages` lists each `{lang,dir}`. Every screen has its page, `headingOutline`, `seoIntent`, `schemaIntent`, `images` with alt text/intent, and `actions` with standalone labels. Empty image/action arrays must match the real page; omission is not evidence of absence.
    36	- `screens[].states` accounts for the seven state names **per language**, with `lang` and `dir`. Applicable states have `page` and `frame:{path,width:1440,version,sha256}`. A non-applicable state has `applicable:false`, `reason`, `reviewer` and `evidence`; default is always applicable. A page-level state may show the relevant component in that state; do not invent a global error screen for a static page.
    37	- Shared component inventory (`components`, `component-consistency`): Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail. Each entry is `{id, variants, states, tokens, behaviour}` (optional `anatomy`, `sizes`, `usage`, `content`); ids are unique, `states` includes `default`, and `tokens` maps each property to a token (it may be empty in the wireframe phase, never at handoff). Every `screens[].components[]` entry names a shared `id`, may choose a declared `variant`, and accounts for its states, whose applicable names must be in the shared definition; a specification field it repeats must equal the shared definition, so one component specified differently on two screens fails. The critic's final round records `componentComparison: {screens, decision, evidence}` covering every screen.
    38	- `screens[].components[].states` also accounts for each state, including justified N/A. Attach appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery in the component specification referenced by the inventory. Every non-default component state must be represented by a real reference frame or state sheet.
    39	- Reachable states beyond the seven (component specification and `interaction-states`): The seven baseline states are a minimum. Derive additional reachable states from actual interactions, including success/completion, selected/checked, expanded/collapsed, validation and read-only where applicable. Specify triggers, appearance, approved copy, semantics, announcements, keyboard/focus behavior and reference evidence. Success communicates the confirmed outcome and next action. The interaction critique fails when a reachable state or transition is unspecified.
    40	- Forms and consequential actions (component specification and `form-behavior`): For every form, specify labels, required/optional indicators, accepted formats, help, validation timing, inline/summary errors, failure focus, input preservation and correction steps. Define submitting, success, failure, retry and duplicate-submission behavior. For consequential actions, specify cancellation, confirmation or undo as appropriate, plus unsaved-change behavior. Each applicable behavior requires approved interface copy and a task-level acceptance check; justify N/A.
    41	- `screens[].acceptance` contains `{id,severity,check,decision,evidence}`. Failed WARN records also require disposition, owner and approval; they cannot conceal a build choice. `screens[].wcag` identifies standard `2.2 AA` and contrast, focus-order and target-size evidence. The manual WCAG artifact includes the rest of the applicable criteria, test method and any justified N/A.
    42	- `copySlots` contains ID, screen, purpose, `kind` (`claim` or `interface`), text, character range, tone, language/direction, placement screenshot, source and claim references. Its `variants` contain actual language/direction/text/source records. `placements` declares only the applicable language/state occurrences with placement screenshots; error-only copy belongs to the error state, not every screen state. Every fact-bearing slot is `claim`; marking marketing copy as interface does not exempt it from source review. Reference HTML marks each visible copy wrapper with `data-copy-slot="ID"`. Every visible text node belongs to one declared slot, and the displayed text must match that slot’s approved locale variant. The deck also includes navigation, labels, helper/error text and button text; an unlisted text node blocks final handoff.
    43	- `facts` contains ID, claim, confirmed status, source path/ref/excerpt/hash and `checkedBy`/`checkedAt`. Cited text must exist at the cited lines. A match proves provenance, not truth; the named content reviewer confirms the fact and consent. Quoted source instructions are never execution authority.
    44	- Wireframe coverage: A wireframe PASS covers every declared desktop screen and locale. Each rendering must declare an existing page and receive the applicable structural/copy, accessibility, integrity and manual checks; missing coverage blocks structure approval. In the wireframe phase every screen's `page` exists, each screen has an applicable `default` state with an existing `page` for every declared language and direction, the checker renders each of them, and the manual records (state `wireframe`) cover every screen in every declared language.
    45	- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.
    46	- `critic.referenceWork` points to the inspected board; `critic.rounds` records up to three rounds, evidence, reviewer, copy checks and design checks. Each check names its critic-rubric row in `row` (`brief-point-of-view`, `copy-truth`, `hierarchy-task-flow`, `typography-language`, `spacing-rhythm-grid`, `colour-imagery-restraint`, `interaction-states-motion` or `accessibility-handoff`), and the final round needs at least one passing, evidenced check per row. The underlying artifact includes the full eight-row rubric, scores and improvement totals. Final checks must all pass; see the critic rubric for the early-stop rule.
    47	- Review records are scoped. Each entry names `screen`, `state` and `lang` of a declared, applicable state and applies to that rendering only; one entry per element (or check) and scope.
    48	  - Optional `reflowExceptions` records the decision for each 2D reflow candidate the 320 px screen reports: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `id` is the candidate element's id and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. An unresolved candidate blocks the final handoff, as does an id that names different elements in one scope.
    49	  - Optional `spacingExceptions` backs each `data-fannan-spacing-exception` on the page: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `reviewer` is the approver. An exception without a matching entry blocks the final handoff.
    50	  - Optional `jsOffDifferences` records an expected JavaScript-off difference: `{check, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `check` is one integrity field (`glass`, `h1`, `placeholders`, `centered`, `typeSizes`, `heroGradient`, `purpleBlue`, `iconRows`, `accent`, `accentRatio`, `maxViewportAccent`, `filler`, `ruleOfThree`, `frames`, `stylesheetsUnscanned`, `spacingOffScale`, `horizontalOverflow`, `clipped`, `offscreenLeft`, `unreachable`, `scrolledText`, `unmeasured`, `unscannedScopes`, `reflowFail`, `contrastFailures`, `contrastUnknown`, `contrastChecked`, `contrastEffects`, `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable`, `textUnmeasured` or `content`) and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. A reason without a reviewer excuses nothing; without a matching entry the difference fails as an integrity mismatch. Different JavaScript-off content (copy slots and headings compared with their text) is never PASS: unexplained it is a mismatch, and with a reviewed `content` entry the entry is recorded but the cross-check stays INCOMPLETE, because the JavaScript-off metrics then prove nothing (R13 N14). Zero JavaScript-off contrast coverage, where the scripted page had coverage, is INCOMPLETE too. The rendered-visibility backstop (`references/design-quality.md`) adds `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable` and `textUnmeasured` per rendering: text obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails, text lost with JavaScript off is a mismatch that a reviewed entry records but never makes PASS, and unmeasurable text is INCOMPLETE. Unresolved compositing on displayed text in either rendering blocks handoff as INCOMPLETE. Token contrast proofs and `jsOffDifferences` cannot waive it. Retaining the effect requires a reviewed measurement implementation that resolves its actual composition for every applicable rendering. Backdrop-filter remains a hard FAIL. The cross-check runs on the positional page and on every declared state; the main gates still fail scri