    13	The automated probes and axe run in a separate browser world (`tools/probe-world.mjs`, a CDP isolated world; Chromium only): they see the page's DOM and CSS but not its JavaScript globals, so a page script cannot patch `getComputedStyle`, `querySelectorAll` or `axe` to hide a failure. `tools/integrity.mjs` cross-checks the positional page: the same probes in the page's own world (a difference is a tampering sign), the page world's probe functions (they must still be native), and a JavaScript-off pass. More glass or a failing 320 px screen with JavaScript off fails as `integrity mismatch` unless the handoff's `jsOffDifferences` records the expected difference with a reason. Text contrast is cross-checked with JavaScript off too: CSS opacity and `filter: opacity()` on the text and its ancestors are composited; any other filter, a blend mode, a backdrop filter or a mask on the chain cannot be resolved, so that text is counted in `contrastEffects` and the cross-check is INCOMPLETE, never a silent pass (the glass hard fail is unchanged). Native control text (input and textarea values or placeholders, button-type input labels, a select's chosen option) is contrast-checked, and the content comparison records each control, link, image and labelled element with its accessible name and displayed value, so a label, value or `aria-label` that only script supplies makes the JavaScript-off content differ.
