    17	Rendered-visibility backstop: the CSS contrast computation is the first pass; what the browser actually painted is the backstop, in both renderings (JavaScript on and off). Every element with its own rendered text, every native control showing text (value, placeholder or chosen option) and every named element without painted text of its own (accessible name only, hit test only) is measured in a scrolled viewport band (every half viewport; the page height is read again in each band) once finite animations have finished and the painted state of every element (opacity, transform, filter, clip-path, colours, position) is unchanged about 100 ms apart, so CSS and script-driven entrances and scroll reveals are measured at rest (bounded at 2 s per band; elements under infinite animations are left out). (a) A hit test at up to three points per text line checks that the text, its own subtree or its ancestor line is on top; a different element that paints there (background colour or image, replaced element, its own text) covers it, and covered at more than half the points is obscured. (b) Pixels: from a viewport screenshot of the same band, the background is the modal colour of the text boxes, ink pixels differ from it by more than 24 in a channel, and the measured ratio is the 90th percentile of the ink pixels' contrast against the background; fewer than three ink pixels, or rendered text under 4 px high, is unreadable, and below 3:1 is unreadable (below 1.5:1 for a target with fewer than 30 ink pixels, such as a single thin glyph that antialiasing never paints at full colour) (a readability floor for the backstop; AA stays with the CSS pass, axe and the manual WCAG review). Text that is obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails; text that is readable with JavaScript on but obscured, unreadable or unmeasurable with it off is an integrity mismatch, and a reviewed `jsOffDifferences` entry for it never makes it PASS. Text the backstop cannot measure (no box, clipped